CommitDate: Sat Nov 7 06:47:37 2026 -0500

    fix: one source for the strict-seed wording so the selftest arm cannot flake (QA-CODE-FACTORY-2)
    
    scripts/verify-node.sh kept a private copy of the checker's --strict-seed sentence
    for its exit-3 branch, and the two differed:
    
      before  scripts/verify-node.sh:219   (--strict-seed: unknown is not a pass)
      after   scripts/node_seed_drift.py   (--strict-seed: an unknown seed is not a pass)
    
    The sentence did not change meaning — the verifier now OBTAINS it instead of
    re-typing it. tests/selftest.sh:4128 greps `an unknown seed is not a pass`, so the
    arm passed only when the seed checker reached exit 1 (an absent record: the
    verifier quotes the checker's own row) and went red whenever the leg was
    unmeasurable (exit 3: 0-byte or unparseable record, whatever --strict-seed says),
    which is the PASS/PASS/FAIL/PASS sequence of 2026-11-06 over four guard runs of
    one hour on one tree. Latent divergence, not a standing red: the suite was green
    at 497ffba before this commit.
    
    - scripts/node_seed_drift.py: STRICT_NOTE is the one owning constant and
      finding_absent builds its row from it, byte-identical output. New --strict-note
      prints just that parenthetical on stdout and exits 0 — a constant, not a
      measurement, so it needs no probe mode. stdlib only, no new dependency.
    - scripts/verify-node.sh: captures the note once when --strict-seed is set and
      appends it in the strict branch; the private copy is gone. No fallback copy: if
      the checker cannot answer, the row says so rather than printing a sentence no
      file owns — a fallback would be the same drift in a new place.
    - tests/selftest.sh, inside the existing "vn" fixture (real verifier, stub
      bunker/curl), three new arms:
        "the strict-seed sentence has one owning source: scripts/node_seed_drift.py"
          — counts the phrase across scripts/*.py|*.sh, so a second copy is a red;
        "a 0-byte record under --strict-seed -> exit 1, FAIL via the verifier's
          exit-3 branch with the checker's sentence" — asserts exit 1,
          `VERIFY: FAIL (1)`, a `FAIL  seed_drift` row, the canonical phrase, and
          `does not parse`, which only the checker's WARN detail carries and so
          proves the run took exit 3 rather than exit 1; the record is restored from
          the pristine copy afterwards, the way the sibling arms do;
        "a scratch tree whose single source lost the phrase -> the arm above goes
          red (exit 1, phrase absent)" — the seeded negative: the same run against a
          scratch copy whose one source was sed-mutated to the old truncated form
          keeps the exit-1 FAIL row and loses the phrase, and the arm FAILs loudly if
          the mutation was a no-op, so it cannot go vacuous.
    
    Sibling checked: verify-node.sh's `(--strict-freshness: unknown is not a pass)`
    has no duplicate to unify — no test greps it (no `strict-freshness` hit anywhere
    under tests/) and scripts/node_freshness.py carries no strict wording of its own
    (grep 'strict' there: no match), so the sentence exists in exactly one place with
    no owning checker to defer to. Left alone on purpose rather than inventing an
    owner for a string only one file prints.
    
    Verified: bash -n scripts/verify-node.sh; python3 -m py_compile
    scripts/node_seed_drift.py; grep -rn 'an unknown seed is not a pass' scripts/ ->
    exactly one hit (scripts/node_seed_drift.py:88); bash tests/selftest.sh ->
    SELFTEST: PASS (exit 0). Not pushed — the foreman pushes.
